## The hold room MWAA does not own

Gate 35, DCA Terminal 2, a weekday morning. The 1997 Pelli National Hall opens into a long, hard-surfaced concourse. American, with roughly 53 percent of DCA departures [FC: trade-press estimate], turns slot-capped narrowbodies against the 67-slot hourly cap.[^19] A gate agent calls Group 4 on a handheld, competing with a muted-but-audible television across the way, a neighboring gate's announcements in a zone the agent cannot silence, and the pier-length HVAC. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back leans in. A passenger without a smartphone watches for the visual cue that is not there. Her boarding pass says Group 3.

MWAA owns the room. It does not own the sound.

That is the operative fact. The Authority controls the shell — ceiling, finishes, base-building PA — and controls tenant fitouts through the Design Manual volumes.[^2] It does not control the airline gate microphone: gate PA inside the leased envelope is airline-run in practice, and whether the executed 2025 U&L names broadcast rights is a GC diligence item [FC/GC: broadcast-rights clause].[^1] It does not control the tenant television, which lives in the concessionaire's lease, or the handler's handheld, which lives in another contract.

None of this is a reason to abandon the standard. It is the reason to write it where the microphones live.

## What the peers actually did

Every airport the trade press calls "silent" governs exceptions. London City, silent since 2008, retains announcements for short-notice gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure.[^20] Helsinki-Vantaa, silent since June 2015, retains gate and emergency calls, and later added banners telling passengers to track time themselves.[^21] Schiphol runs "virtually no announcements" alongside a formal accessibility program.[^22] Copenhagen zones quiet by gate area.[^23] San Francisco, the only documented U.S. large-hub program, reports a 40 percent cut in boarding-area announcements and 90 minutes a day removed from the International Terminal — alongside a public record of complaints about missed information.[^24] Chennai's 2017 rollout drew a backlash serious enough that officials conceded "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged are available."[^10]

No peer has published measured missed-boarding, missed-connection, or IROPS data attributable to a silent-airport policy. An argument from peer branding is design conservatism, and it should be defended as such. Notice, too, that every one of these programs is an exception-governance regime rather than the absence of a PA. The variable at MWAA is not how much silence to inherit. It is what the exceptions are, who authorizes them, and in which zone.

## The floors the standard inherits — and the one it adds

The standard inherits its floors. For emergency communication, NFPA 72 requires an STI of 0.45 at 90 percent of locations in each Acoustically Distinguishable Space, averaging 0.50.[^3] The code permits instrumented STIPA verification but does not require it; a listening test satisfies it. That gap is the affirmative case for a design-manual amendment. Only the owner specification can elevate ACRP 175's targets — STI 0.50 for terminal PA, 10 dB(A) of signal-to-noise margin in pier hold rooms [FC: verify against primary PDF] — to required performance, and only the owner specification can mandate occupied-condition STIPA at commissioning.[^4] The Airports Authority Fire Marshal, the AHJ inside the federal-leased perimeter [FC/GC: written delegation unconfirmed], enforces the code floor at certificate of occupancy; the owner specification enforces the delta above it.[^5]

The second floor is accessibility: three regulations, three actors. 14 CFR Part 382 binds carriers on facilities they control, including captionable televisions.[^6] Section 504 and 49 CFR Part 27 bind MWAA on airport-controlled communication;[^7] 28 CFR §35.160 adds "primary consideration" of a passenger's requested aid.[^8] The concessionaire's television sits in a gap no federal regulation squarely occupies — which is why the tenant handbook is the lever, and why DOT's rulemaking toward real-time accessible information matters even where Part 382 does not reach [FC: final-rule status].[^9] The redundant-channel floor also protects grant assurance under 49 U.S.C. §47107 where grant funds touch the stack.

## Two airports, two calibrations

DCA closed 2025 at 24.9 million passengers — down 5.3 percent, its lowest since 2022 — absorbing the January midair collision and a seven-week fall shutdown.[^25] It runs one runway capped at 67 hourly slots; the 2024 FAA Reauthorization added ten beyond-perimeter exemptions in December 2024.[^11]

Do not price the DCA argument in denied-boarding dollars. A passenger who misses a boarding call is a no-show — under 1 percent of boardings per GAO, the cost mostly the passenger's.[^26] The DCA argument is exposure. Slot scarcity makes a schedule-level miss asymmetric in a way it is not at IAD, and §9.05.8 monetizes that asymmetry at $1 million per beyond-perimeter pair, conditioned on a $2 CPE advantage. The current gap is $2.36 [FC: §9.05.8 rests on consultant analysis, not public text], leaving $0.36 of headroom; the standard's job is to keep DCA's audible channel reliable enough that it never starts consuming it.[^12] The delivery vehicle is Terminal 2's gate-area renovations inside the $2.39 billion DCA CCP, reached through the program brief while the design is open.[^27]

IAD ran roughly 29 million passengers in 2025, 10.5 million international — both records.[^28] Its journeys are longer, its populations more multilingual, its transfers more dependent on visual and digital redundancy than on English audio. So calibrate IAD for the visual and digital channels and DCA for the audible one: a common intelligibility floor, with an explicit DCA variance for announcement density and IROPS PA envelope.

## The counter-case, honestly

The strongest argument against a new standard is that MWAA does not need one. NFPA 72, ADA Title II, the ACAA, and a competent commissioning specification already require most of it; a consultant-scope paragraph requiring occupied-condition STIPA would deliver most of the value without a board resolution. The Board has not asked for this. And the January 2025 midair is still in memory; a "quiet" framing will be read against it.

The standard concedes three things. It is not a new requirement but the integration layer across requirements that today live in different consultants' scopes on different projects with no owner for the seam. It never carries the word "quiet" in an external MWAA document — the Schiphol and Helsinki pattern is to brand silence and govern intelligibility — so it is the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard. And it says nothing on Day 1 about airline announcement scripting inside the leased envelope. That is a 2039 U&L review target, staged through bilateral MOUs with United at IAD and American at DCA, negotiated on MWAA's data rather than through a lease-exhibit fight the Authority does not need now.

What it does say: what the base building must achieve, what the tenant volumes add, what each signatory MOU commits to on gate-PA discipline, and what commissioning must verify before substantial completion. Berlin Brandenburg shows what happens when that layer is missing — a life-safety and systems-integration failure against a frozen design, €2 billion closing at €7.3 billion, nine lost years.[^29] Denver's baggage system is the same class of failure: $193 million past $400 million, abandoned in 2005.[^30] Both were preventable at design.

The 17-seat Board splits three ways: a seven-seat Virginia majority oriented to the Dulles program, D.C. and Maryland appointees who hear from the disability communities most exposed to a reduced-information framing, and federal appointees carrying the July 2026 White House and USDOT posture. A safety-and-accessibility performance floor is legible to all three. A "quiet" resolution is not.[^31] On the airline side, use-and-lease majority-in-interest carve-outs commonly exempt safety- and security-mandated capital, which is why the standard must sit inside that carve-out from the first draft, conditioned on GC confirming the executed 2025 U&L follows the pattern [FC/GC: MII carve-out unverified].[^32]

## Where it gets written, and by when

Adopt the standard as a Design Manual amendment, executed by the VP of Design and Construction under existing delegated authority, with a concurrent information item to the Board's Business Administration Committee — a new performance section in Volume 1 and the tenant volumes, inside the FY26 revision cycle.[^2] Convert to a Board resolution only if GC's MII memo finds it materially alters airline rates and charges.

The mandatory floor at both airports: NFPA 72 STI verified by IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions; ACRP 175 signal-to-noise margin in every pier hold room; a redundant-channel obligation — audible, visual/digital, staffed — for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message; and PA-push authority anchored to a named position. Add an emergency-notification stack on generator, UPS, and local battery, with a duty-officer analog fallback for the day the managed-IT layer fails, as on 19 July 2024;[^33] a 60-second FIDS SLA from AODB update to gate-change display; and an IROPS clause permitting temporary relaxation under duty-officer sign-off, exercised annually. Recommended practice: NC/RC targets by space type, quiet-zone locations, and annual OSHA 1910.95 dosimetry at the ten noisiest locations per airport.[^34] Context-dependent: the DCA variance, plus SHPO-consulted preservation carve-outs for the Saarinen Main Terminal and DCA's 1941 Terminal 1 — defined character-defining features, not blanket exemptions.

Funding sits in four places. Design fees ride existing consultant budgets on the C/D and E lines. The construction premium is incremental scope inside the $20–22.5 billion envelope, financed through the CCP GARB stack. The mobile-notification platform is an IT acquisition — 2 CFR Part 200 and DBE if grant-funded — pending the CIO's finding on whether the existing stack extends before any 9-to-18-month procurement. Lifecycle O&M, $600 thousand to $1.8 million a year, flows through the operating budget and recovers through the airline O&M line.[^18]

The President and CEO owns it; the COO, VP D&C, and VP Airline Affairs co-sponsor. The first 90 days: GC returns the MII memo and confirms the AHJ delegation in writing; VP D&C convenes the working group and signs the Concourse E acoustics-and-commissioning contract inside 30 days, or the baseline recasts to first C/D substantial completion; VP Airline Affairs pre-briefs United and American; the CIO returns the platform finding; the Airports VPs open the tenant-handbook amendment on the concession renewal schedule.

Leading indicators are process inputs: C/D deliverables with acoustic sections, renewals on amended handbook language, signatory meetings held, FY27 scopes naming the standard. The acceptance tests — STIPA at commissioning and again 60 days after opening — judge them.

Stop conditions, each owned and dated: the MII carve-out does not cover the standard — convert to a signatory side letter (GC, Day 45); the AHJ declines occupied-condition STIPA — negotiate an alternative protocol (Fire Marshal, Day 60); a signatory formally objects to the DCA framing — drop the variance (VP Airline Affairs, Day 90); the DOT final rule contradicts a clause — amend on publication (GC); the Concourse E baseline lands inside the ACRP band — defer C/D application (VP D&C, Day 120 after opening); FY27 budgets cannot absorb the O&M line (CFO, at markup). The failure mode to guard against is scope migration into an announcement-policy standard the airlines contest under the Use and Lease.

Labor is owned: DCA's higher floor means more voice work at American's gates — a CBA matter for American, a training matter for MWAA staff, with a plan inside 180 days. The bond-covenant test is trivial at this sizing; Treasury confirms in routine rating dialogue.

## A last image

Return to Gate 35. The ceiling and wall absorption are different; the base-building loudspeakers reach every seat at an STI the Fire Marshal will accept in occupied conditions. The airline gate PA is unchanged; American still calls Group 3. The concessionaire's television is captioned and muted per the amended handbook — reaching the full portfolio only as leases renew, a horizon, not a date. The FIDS updates within 60 seconds. The passenger with hearing aids hears the call cleanly; the passenger without a smartphone reads the same information off a display. The gate agent, no longer fighting adjacent-zone spillover, spends four minutes with the family that missed the sequence instead of with the microphone.

Nothing in that room is quiet in the marketing sense. Every message that matters lands. That is the standard MWAA can write, and the only one worth writing before the concrete cures.

